<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hilandar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1198">1198</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Chilandar, Hilandar Monastery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hilandar is a Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, founded in 1198 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>, then the monk Simeon, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. Richly endowed by Serbian rulers, it became the leading centre of Serbian religious life and literature in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and holds a great library of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and icons. It is one of the twenty ruling monasteries of Athos.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilandar.jpg" alt="Hilandar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Саша Шљукић · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Hilandar_monastery,_Mount_Athos,_Greece.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
